Dermatitis Herpetiformis (Mild Localised)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Small grouped papules on elbows/buttocks, related to latent gluten sensitivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-012
Reason for Visit: Acute, intractable pruritus causing severe sleep deprivation and secondary excoriation risk.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate an acutely pruritic, spreading vesicular rash. Prompt clinical differentiation from highly infectious scabies or early herpes zoster was strictly required to prevent institutional/household transmission and manage severe distress prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: These blisters on my elbows are itching so intensely I feel like tearing my skin off, and I haven't slept a wink.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports extremely mild, isolated itchy vesicles starting 24 hours ago, which rapidly evolved into severe, unrelenting pruritus. The timeline shows a progression from a mild burning sensation to the eruption of grouped vesicles. The patient has been scratching aggressively, leading to raw, excoriated skin, and expresses acute distress and fear of a highly contagious infestation like scabies.
Functional Status: Independent, extremely fatigued from sleep deprivation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living in a shared household, high anxiety regarding contagion.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, visibly distressed, continuously attempting to scratch extensor surfaces.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals small grouped papules on elbows/buttocks, related to latent gluten sensitivity. Multiple lesions are heavily excoriated, leaving pinpoint crusts. No burrows evident in interdigital web spaces. No dermatomal distribution. No cellulitic erythema or purulent discharge surrounding the raw areas.
Environmental Observations: Clean living space, no obvious environmental triggers or close contacts with similar symptoms.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No current medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Dermatitis Herpetiformis (Mild Localised) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Scabies - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of characteristic burrows in the web spaces of fingers and toes, and lack of similar symptoms in close household contacts.

Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, bilateral presentation on extensor surfaces rather than a unilateral, dermatomal distribution.

Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Classic clustering of vesicles on specific extensor surfaces (elbows, buttocks) without a clear history of a novel topical exposure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented in severe distress with an acutely pruritic vesicular rash. Triage correctly prioritized the case to exclude an infectious etiology (scabies, zoster) that would necessitate immediate isolation protocols in a shared living arrangement. Clinical examination confirmed the symmetric, extensor-predominant nature of the lesions, consistent with an autoimmune cutaneous manifestation of gluten sensitivity rather than an infectious agent. This assessment was essential to safely initiate targeted topical therapy, provide infection control clearance, and alleviate acute sleep deprivation, justifying urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-potency topical corticosteroid ointment and sedating antihistamine (promethazine) for nocturnal symptom control.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply the steroid ointment directly to the lesions and take the antihistamine precisely 30 minutes before bed. By aggressively suppressing the inflammatory itch cascade right before sleep, you will achieve unbroken rest, breaking the scratch-itch cycle and allowing the skin to heal.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7 days for Coeliac serology screening.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to a GP clinic or Emergency Department if the excoriated areas become increasingly red, hot to the touch, or begin draining yellow pus, indicating a secondary bacterial infection.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending skin biopsy and serology; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The intensely itchy blisters are an autoimmune reaction, often linked to dietary gluten. Topical steroids provide rapid relief, but strict diet modification is the long-term cure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the rash spreads rapidly to your face or inside your mouth, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is reacting in the skin, causing the severe itch. It is completely non-contagious. The nighttime medication will stop the itch and help you sleep.
Actionable Steps: Keep your fingernails cut short to prevent further skin damage. Do not scrub the areas in the shower.

